WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
SANDEEP DINESH: When a pod
is scheduled by Kubernetes,

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:04.710
it's important the containers
have enough resources

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.210
to actually run.

00:00:06.210 --> 00:00:09.990
If you schedule a large app on
a node with limited resources,

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:12.690
it's possible the node
runs out of memory or CPU

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.820
and things just stop working.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:17.170
In this episode of
"Kubernetes Best Practices,"

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:19.628
let's take a look at how you
can solve these problems using

00:00:19.628 --> 00:00:21.386
resource requests and limits.

00:00:21.386 --> 00:00:24.242
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:31.030
Requests and limits
are the mechanisms

00:00:31.030 --> 00:00:33.280
Kubernetes uses to
control resources

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.750
such as CPU and memory.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:39.570
Requests are what the
container is guaranteed to get.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.440
If a container
requests a resource,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:43.640
Kubernetes will only
schedule it on a node

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:46.100
they can give it that resource.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.740
Limits, on the other hand,
make sure a container never

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:50.630
goes above a value.

00:00:50.630 --> 00:00:53.330
The container is only
allowed to go up to limit

00:00:53.330 --> 00:00:55.320
and then its restricted.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.500
Let's see how these work.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.140
So there are two
types of resources--

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:01.170
CPU and memory.

00:01:01.170 --> 00:01:03.310
The Kubernetes scheduler
uses these to figure out

00:01:03.310 --> 00:01:05.459
where to run your pods.

00:01:05.459 --> 00:01:08.430
A typical spec for
pod for resources

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.610
might look something like this.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:13.340
Each container in the pod
can set its own requests

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:16.500
and limits, and these
are all additive.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.640
CPU resources are
defined in millicores.

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:22.320
If your container needs
two full cores to run,

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.650
you'd put the value 2000m.

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.170
If your container only
needs 1/4 of a core,

00:01:27.170 --> 00:01:30.200
you would put a value of 250m.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.000
One thing to keep in
mind is that if you

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:34.370
put a value in that's
larger than the core count

00:01:34.370 --> 00:01:38.330
of your biggest node, then your
pod will never be scheduled.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:40.670
Let's say you have a pod
that needs four cores,

00:01:40.670 --> 00:01:44.000
but your Kubernetes cluster is
just comprised of two core VMs.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.440
In this case, your pod
will never be scheduled.

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:49.210
So unless your app
is specifically

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:51.670
designed to take advantage
of multiple cores--

00:01:51.670 --> 00:01:53.050
things like
scientific computing,

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:54.660
and some databases
come to mind--

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:56.860
it's usually a best
practice to keep the CPU

00:01:56.860 --> 00:01:59.810
request at one or below,
and then run more replicas

00:01:59.810 --> 00:02:01.340
to scale it out.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:03.040
This gives the system
more flexibility

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:04.780
and more reliability.

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:08.110
When it comes to CPU limits,
things get interesting.

00:02:08.110 --> 00:02:11.080
So CPU is consider a
compressible resource.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.450
If your app starts
hitting your CPU limits,

00:02:13.450 --> 00:02:16.210
Kubernetes will start to
throttle your container.

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:19.050
This means your CPU will
be artificially restricted,

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.660
giving your app potentially
worse performance.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.610
However, it won't be
terminated or evicted.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:27.370
Memory resources are
defined in bytes.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:29.980
Normally, you give a
mebibyte value from memory.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:33.460
But you can give it anything
from bytes to petabytes.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:36.190
Just like CPU, if you put
a memory request that's

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:38.620
larger than the amount
of memory on your nodes,

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.860
the pod will never be scheduled.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:45.490
Now, unlike CPU resources,
memory is not compressible.

00:02:45.490 --> 00:02:48.160
Because there's no way
to throttle memory usage,

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.560
if a container goes
past its memory limit,

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:52.270
it will be terminated.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:55.150
It's important to remember that
you cannot set requests that

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:58.210
are larger than the resources
provided by your nodes.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:02.980
You can find the total resources
for GKE VMs at this link.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.170
In an ideal world,
the container settings

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:07.580
would be good enough to
take care of everything.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:09.730
But the world is a dark
and terrible place.

00:03:09.730 --> 00:03:10.688
[THUNDER AND LIGHTNING]

00:03:10.688 --> 00:03:12.910
People can easily forget
to set the resources,

00:03:12.910 --> 00:03:16.360
or a rogue team can set their
requests and limits very high

00:03:16.360 --> 00:03:19.450
and take up more than their
fair share of the cluster.

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:22.750
To prevent these scenarios,
you can set up resource quotas

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.880
and limit ranges.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:26.980
After creating a namespace,
you can lock them

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:28.690
down using quotas.

00:03:28.690 --> 00:03:31.750
For example, if you have a
production and development

00:03:31.750 --> 00:03:35.740
namespace, a common pattern is
to put no quota on production

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:39.089
and then put very strict quotas
on the development namespace.

00:03:39.089 --> 00:03:41.380
This allows production to
take all the resource that it

00:03:41.380 --> 00:03:44.020
needs in case of a spike
in traffic and development

00:03:44.020 --> 00:03:45.790
is lockdown.

00:03:45.790 --> 00:03:49.660
A quota for resources might
look something like this.

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:52.500
In this example, you can see
that there are four sections.

00:03:52.500 --> 00:03:54.390
Let's go into each one.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:58.200
Requested CPU is the maximum
combined CPU requests

00:03:58.200 --> 00:04:01.300
that all containers in
the namespace can have.

00:04:01.300 --> 00:04:03.630
So in this example, you
can have 50 containers

00:04:03.630 --> 00:04:07.470
with 10m requests, five
containers with 100m requests,

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:10.860
or just one container
with 500m's of requests.

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.710
As long as the total
requested CPU in the namespace

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:17.170
is less than 500m,
we're good to go.

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:20.350
request.memory is the
maximum combined memory

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:23.230
requests that all containers
in the namespace can have.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:24.750
So again, in the
above example, you

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:27.730
can have 50 containers
with two MB requests,

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:29.710
five containers
or 20 MB requests,

00:04:29.710 --> 00:04:32.020
or just one container with 100--

00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:35.080
as long as the total requested
memory in the namespace

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:38.120
is less than 100 mebibytes.

00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.732
Limits.cpu Is a maximum
combined speed limits

00:04:40.732 --> 00:04:42.690
that all the containers
and namespace can have.

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.410
It's just like requests.cpu,
but for the limits.

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:48.980
And then finally, limits.memory
is the maximum combined memory

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:51.260
limits that all containers
in the namespace can have.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:04:54.260
Again, just like the
request,memory but for a limit

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:55.540
instead.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:59.120
You can also create a limit
range in your namespace.

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.860
Unlike a quota, which looks
at the whole namespace,

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:05.675
a limit range enforces itself
on individual containers.

00:05:05.675 --> 00:05:07.550
So this can help prevent
people from creating

00:05:07.550 --> 00:05:09.830
super tiny or super
large containers

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:11.510
inside the namespace.

00:05:11.510 --> 00:05:14.084
A limit range might look
something like this.

00:05:14.084 --> 00:05:16.250
So looking at the example,
you can see again there's

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:17.450
four sections.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:19.910
Let's go into each one.

00:05:19.910 --> 00:05:22.670
The default section will
set up the default limits

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:24.740
for a container in the pod.

00:05:24.740 --> 00:05:27.920
If you set these values in the
limit range, any containers

00:05:27.920 --> 00:05:29.630
that don't explicitly
set these values

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:33.360
themselves will get
assigned the default values.

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.530
The default request section
will set up the default requests

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:38.330
for a container in a pod.

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:40.880
Again, if you set these
values in the limit range,

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.010
any containers that don't
explicitly set these

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:47.080
themselves will get assigned
these default values.

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:50.140
The max section will set
up the maximum limits

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:52.640
that a container
in a pod can set.

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:56.150
The default section cannot be
set higher than this value,

00:05:56.150 --> 00:05:59.670
and the limits in a container
cannot be higher as well.

00:05:59.670 --> 00:06:02.200
It's important to note
that if this value is set

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:04.550
and the default
section is not set,

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:08.770
the max value becomes the
default value as well.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:11.970
The min section will set up the
minimum requests of a container

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:13.620
and a pod can set.

00:06:13.620 --> 00:06:16.920
The default requests section
cannot be lower than this,

00:06:16.920 --> 00:06:19.170
and requests that are on a
container cannot be lower

00:06:19.170 --> 00:06:20.590
as well.

00:06:20.590 --> 00:06:23.610
Again, it's important to note
that if this value is set

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:26.130
and the default requests
section is not set,

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:30.400
the minimum value becomes
the default request as well.

00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:33.060
So at the end of the day,
these resources requests

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.770
are used by the
Kubernetes scheduler

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:35.790
to run your workloads.

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.620
And it's kind of
important to understand

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:41.320
how this works so you can tune
your containers correctly.

00:06:41.320 --> 00:06:44.610
So let's say you want to run
some pods on your cluster.

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:47.160
Assuming the pod
specifications are valid,

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.410
the Kubernetes schedule
will use round robin load

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:52.130
balancing to pick a node
to run your workload.

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:54.660
So Kubernetes will check if
the node has enough resources

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:57.120
to fulfill the request
on the pod's containers.

00:06:57.120 --> 00:07:00.540
If it doesn't, then it'll
move on to the next node.

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:02.040
If none of the
nodes in the system

00:07:02.040 --> 00:07:04.500
have resources left
to fill the requests,

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:07.110
then pods go into
a pending state.

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:09.300
By using Google Kubernetes
engine's features,

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.600
such as the node autoscaler,
GKE can automatically

00:07:12.600 --> 00:07:16.350
detect a state and then create
more nodes automatically.

00:07:16.350 --> 00:07:18.450
And then if there's an
excess capacity of nodes,

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:20.730
the autoscaler can
scale it down and remove

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:23.290
nodes to save you money.

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:26.980
So Kubernetes schedule these
pods based on the requests.

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:30.370
But a limit can be higher
than the requests, right?

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:32.800
So this means that
in some scenarios

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.820
a node can actually
run out of resources.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.830
And we call this an
overcommitted state.

00:07:38.830 --> 00:07:41.170
So when it comes to CPU,
like we said before,

00:07:41.170 --> 00:07:43.210
Kubernetes will start
to throttle the pods.

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:45.250
Each pod will get as
much as it requested,

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:47.260
but it might not be
able to go up the limit.

00:07:47.260 --> 00:07:49.310
We'll start throttling it down.

00:07:49.310 --> 00:07:51.340
But when it comes to
memory, Kubernetes

00:07:51.340 --> 00:07:54.460
has to make some decisions
on which pods to kill

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:57.520
and which part to keep until
you free up system resources.

00:07:57.520 --> 00:08:00.170
Otherwise, the whole
system will crash.

00:08:00.170 --> 00:08:02.350
So let's imagine a scenario
where you have a machine

00:08:02.350 --> 00:08:04.100
and that's kind of
running out of memory--

00:08:04.100 --> 00:08:05.936
what will Kubernetes do?

00:08:05.936 --> 00:08:08.620
So Kubernetes will
look for pods that

00:08:08.620 --> 00:08:11.840
are using more resources
than they requested.

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:13.965
So if your containers have
no requests at all, then

00:08:13.965 --> 00:08:16.048
by default they're using
more than they requested,

00:08:16.048 --> 00:08:17.410
because they requested nothing.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:20.410
So these are prime
candidates for termination.

00:08:20.410 --> 00:08:22.330
Another prime candidate
are containers

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:24.310
that have gone
over their requests

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:26.524
but are still under the limit.

00:08:26.524 --> 00:08:28.232
So if Kubernetes finds
multiple pods that

00:08:28.232 --> 00:08:30.550
have gone over their
request, then Kubernetes

00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:32.710
will rank these
pods by priority,

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:36.000
and then terminate the
lowest priority pods first.

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:38.662
If all the pod have
the same priority,

00:08:38.662 --> 00:08:40.120
then Kubernetes
terminates the pods

00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:43.090
that have gone the
most over its request.

00:08:43.090 --> 00:08:45.490
In very rare
scenarios, Kubernetes

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:47.800
might be forced to terminate
pods that are still

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:49.330
within their requests.

00:08:49.330 --> 00:08:51.760
This can happen when
critical system components,

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.880
like the kubelet or Docker,
start taking more resources

00:08:54.880 --> 00:08:56.717
than were reserved for them.

00:08:56.717 --> 00:08:58.300
So while your
Kubernetes cluster might

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:01.640
work fine without setting
resource requests and limits,

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.060
you're going to start running
into more and more issues

00:09:04.060 --> 00:09:06.760
as your teams and projects
start to grow larger.

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:10.010
Adding requests and limits
to your pods and namespaces

00:09:10.010 --> 00:09:12.154
only takes a little
extra effort,

00:09:12.154 --> 00:09:14.320
and it can save you from
running into many headaches

00:09:14.320 --> 00:09:15.820
down the line.

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:18.790
I'll see you on the next episode
of "Kubernetes Best Practices."

00:09:18.790 --> 00:09:22.140
[MUSIC PLAYING]

